    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's dive into the engineering side of things.\n\nThe tensile strength of steel is typically much higher than the compressive strength of concrete, which means that steel can withstand significant tensile forces without failing, while concrete is more prone to cracking under tensile stress. This is because steel has a higher yield strength and ultimate tensile strength than concrete.\n\nThink of it like a rubber band - the rubber band can stretch a lot before breaking, but it's still relatively weak in terms of compressive strength. Concrete, on the other hand, is like a dense, heavy brick that can withstand a lot of compressive force, but it's much more brittle and prone to cracking under tensile stress.\n\nIn terms of the actual math, the relationship between the compressive strength of concrete (f'c) and the tensile strength of steel (f'y) is often described by the following equation:\n\nf'y / f'c = σ / σ'c\n\nwhere σ is the tensile stress and σ'c is the compressive stress. This equation shows that the tensile strength of steel is typically much higher than the compressive strength of concrete, which is why rebar is",
